## ABSTRACT
Expanding pore size range of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) promotes their versatility and feasibility for various biomedical applications. However, natural pore size greatly restricts large guest molecule accommodation. Customizing and tailoring pore apertures ranging from micropores to mesopores controllably is desired but still critically challenging. Herein, we developed a facile method with super mildness based on pH-sensitive zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF)-8 to increase porosity, providing pore size with maximum \(20\ \mathsf{n m}\), which is 8 times larger than average. Glucose oxidase (GOx) was introduced in ZIF-8 for bioetching, benefitted from the resultant acidic micro-nvironment during biocatalytic process. Different synthesis methods were assessed for obtaining different morphologies and size distributions. Reaction time, GOx encapsulation efficiency, and \(Z\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) concentration was optimized to precisely control the mesopore size distribution of MOFs. It was found that bio-etching strategy was capable of producing stable mesopores which were large enough for loading lactase with good enzymatic activity retained, verified both in vitro and in vivo. This strategy breaks natural pore size limitation of MOFs and thereby facilitates biomolecule delivery, catalysis, and other biomedical applications with enhanced stability and performance.
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic frameworks, enzyme immobilization, drug delivery, oral biocompatibility
## 1 Introduction
In the past decades, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention. Intrinsically, diversity of structure and chemical composition renders controllable design of MOFs for different applications, such as gas adsorption/separation [1], catalysis [2, 3], sensing [4, 5], imaging [6], energy storage [7, 8], drug delivery [9–12], and waste management [13, 14]. Moreover, large surface areas and inherent porosity make MOFs possess great potentials as drug carriers after loading therapeutic cargos [15]. Since therapeutic drugs involve in small molecules, peptides, proteins, and polysaccharide, the regulation of pore size distribution offers more flexibility for drug encapsulation, protection, and selectivity, which is desirable yet still challenging.
Structure designability and pore tunability are the most highlighted features of MOFs. Regarding the pore size of MOFs, it can be adjusted to develop a series of MOFs using ligands with different chains. According to previous results, the pore sizes of most reported MOFs are usually limited to micropore range with pore diameter smaller than \(2\ \mathsf{n m}\) [16]. Theoretically, the pore of MOFs can be increased by expanding the length of linkers isoreticularly [17]. Yaghi et al. [18] developed a series of isoreticular linkers ranging from 7.0 to \(50\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) for building IRMOF-74 with the maximum pore size of \(98\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) (IRMOF-74-XI). However, the elongation of ligands is not unlimited due to
interpenetrated structures [19]. Additionally, the structure may collapse once solvents or guest molecules are removed from pores caused by undermined stability of MOFs, which weakens practical value in biomedical applications [20].
