Herein, we developed a micro-etching strategy based on a pHsensitive MOF, namely zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF)-8, to controllably increase porosity as well as pore size. Avci et al. developed a wet-chemical etching method to obtain enlarged pores by adjusting the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the etchant solution [16]. But the etched pores are \(\sim1~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) which has risks in biomolecule leakage. Since the development of MOFs with mesopores \((2{-}50~\mathrm{\nm})\) is required by macromolecule \((>\ 2\ \mathrm{\nm})\) encapsulation and protection, such as enzymes, Cas 9, and other therapeutic
macromolecules, we expanded uniform mesopores in MOFs, which is beneficial for efficient biomolecules encapsulation.
Three key points are involved in this work: (1) mild low-pH environment for creating uniform mesopores; (2) controllable pore size after micro-etching to avoid oversized pores; (3) maintained porosity for macromolecule encapsulation with activities. To address this problem, glucose oxidase (GOx) was employed in ZIF-8 particles in order to reduce \(\mathrm{pH}\) through oxidation in the existence of glucose in a mild way. Therefore, micro-etching can be controlled by changing adjustable GOx encapsulation process (Scheme 1).
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Size and morphology control of ZIF-8
ZIF-8 particles were synthesized through three different methods for screening appropriate synthesis strategy. Figure 1 presents the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, and particles size of as-synthesized ZIF-8 particles. It is worth noting that all three methods were conducted at room temperature without using dimethylformamide (DMF), which are green synthesis routes and energy-saving. Moreover, high synthesis temperature and organic solvents may result in denature of biomolecules in the process of encapsulating GOx.
Compared with traditional ratio \((Z\mathbf{n}^{2+};\mathbf{ligand}~=~1;1)\), the repressive molar ratio of 2-methylimidazole (Hmin) to \(Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) was set to 700:1, where Hmin was tremendously excessive. Hmin/Zn molar ratio is an important parameter for ZIF-8 size control, which has been confirmed by Ref. [25]. Kida and coauthors [26] adjusted the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Hmin}/\mathrm{Zn}\) from 20:1 to 100:1 and investigated its influence on particle size. It was verified that higher molar ratio resulted in significant decrease in ZIF-8 size. Inspired by this strategy, we adjusted the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Hmin}/\mathrm{Zn}\) to 700:1 for decreasing the particle size to approximate \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\). In this condition, excess ligand hindered the growth of ZIF-8 particles, and the particles size remained around \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\). Zhang and coworkers [27] claimed that an excess of Hmin could result in aggregation when the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Hmin}/\mathrm{Zn}\) was 16:1. Contrary to previous results, we found that when the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Hmin}/\mathrm{Zn}\) increased to 700:1, the particle size decreased from \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) to \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) with good dispersion (Figs. 1(a)–1(d)). Figure 1(h) showed that the average size of ZIF-8 synthesized both in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and EtOH were \(136\pm5\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(139\pm2\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively. The polydispersity indexes (PDI)s of as-prepared ZIF-8 particles synthesized in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and EtOH without cetrimonium bromide (CTAB) measured from dynamic light scattering (DLS) are 0.184 and 0.033, respectively, verifying that the particle size distribution
was narrower with better dispersion when the synthesis was conducted in EtOH, which can also be confirmed by TEM images (Figs. 1(c) and 1(d)). So dispersibility of the obtained ZIF-8 nanoparticles was improved, indicating that solvent can affect the crystallization process. When the solvent was \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), with the addition of CTAB, the morphology of ZIF-8 was amazingly changed to flower-like as shown in Figs. 1(e) and 1(f), whereas the size increased to \(2.5\pm0.3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\).
PXRD was employed to verify the composition and structural information of as-synthesized ZIF-8 particles. According to simulated PXRD pattern of ZIF-8, several characteristic diffraction peaks are shown in Fig. 1(g). The peaks at \(2\theta=7.4^{\circ}\), \(10.5^{\circ}\), \(12.8^{\circ}\), \(14.8^{\circ}\), \(16.6^{\circ}\), \(18.2^{\circ}\), and \(19.7^{\circ}\) correspond to planes of (011), (002), (112), (022), (013), (222), and (123), respectively [28, 29]. Both the materials synthesized in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and EtOH matched the simulated peaks very well. However, XRD patterns of ZIF-8 (CTAB added) did not exhibit neat peaks and two characteristic peaks of ZIF-8 (\(2\theta=7.4^{\circ}\) and \(10.5^{\circ}\)) were greatly weakened, whereas shoulder peaks ascribed to crystal orientation formed. It is supposed to be crystal growth orientation induced by CTAB [30, 31] since surfactant molecules adsorb preferentially onto specific facets. In this case, CTAB hindered growth of (011) and (002) planes. Meanwhile, the crystal growth rate and orientation were adjusted, resulting in self-assembly and heterostructures. Uncharacteristically, high-index surfaces showed higher intensity than low-index ones. This could be caused by stabilization of long hydrophobic hydrocarbon chains of CTAB. Considering particle size and dispersion controllability, CTAB was not added in the following enzyme encapsulation.
## 2.2 Synthesis of GOx@ZIF-8
Given ZIF-8 particles synthesized in EtOH showed best crystallinity and dispersion, they were used for encapsulating GOx for micro-etching after adding glucose. In detail, uniform ZIF-8 nanoparticles acted as a core with a controllable layered ZIF-8 coating by changing the concentration of \(Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) when the second layer was growing. In the meanwhile, GOx were encapsulated between the two layers for building uniform mesopores. Employing ZIF-8 particle as a seed with the second-round growth offered increased particle size which was confirmed by both TEM images and DLS data. Figure 2(a) demonstrated that there was no big difference in particle sizes when reaction time ranged from 1 to 30 min, indicating that the coating could be finished efficiently within 1 min. Slight fluctuation bottomed at 250 nm with the smallest PDI (0.075). Besides, size dispersity of the material with 10-min coating exhibited satisfactory result, which was confirmed by Figs. 2(c)–2(f). The morphology of ZIF-8 synthesized in EtOH was well-defined rhombic dodecahedral while the edge of GOx
carrying ZIF-8 particles was blurred due to quick formation, as shown in TEM images (Figs. 2(c)–2(f)).
We further evaluated the capability of encapsulating GOx, which is critical for bio-etching as GOx converts glucose and oxygen to gluconolactone and hydrogen peroxide [32], thus changing the micro-environment for etching. In the process of
ZIF-8 coating on the core, GOx was added for in situ encapsulation. According to calculation from BCA assay results, the encapsulation efficiency increased with the amount of GOx. Only \(40\%\) GOx could be embedded in ZIF-8 when \(50~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{GOx}\) was added and this figure increased to \(91\%\) with GOx amount increasing to 10 times (Fig. 3(a)). It suggested that the encapsulated amount of GOx was enhanced when more GOx was initially added. GOx is a dimeric globular protein, and its 3D dimension is \(6.0\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,5.2\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,7.7\ \mathrm{nm}\) [33]. Thus, the ZIF-8 coating for GOx encapsulation is based on heterogeneous nucleation. It was supposed that the GOx provided additional nucleation sites to promote heterogeneous growth of ZIF-8 coating, which is consistent with the results published by Lee and coauthors [34]. Therefore, the enhanced heterogeneous nucleation posed a positive impact on the encapsulation efficiency of GOx. Correspondingly, the maximum encapsulation capability reached up to \(1.14~\mathrm{mg\cdot mg^{-1}}\) while in the case of \(50~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{GOx}\) added, the capability was \(0.05\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mg^{-1}}\) (Fig. 3(b)).
## 2.3 Synthesis of mesoporous ZIF-8 by micro-etching
It is known that the reaction mechanism of GOx is the oxidation of D-glucose to glucono-δ-lactone and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), where \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) acts as an electron acceptor with resultant acidic environment [35]. Resultantly, low \(\mathrm{pH}\) solution is a good etching source for ZIF-8 [36]. Herein, for the sake of better understanding relationship between etching layer thickness and GOx amount, we used ZIF-8 as seeds around which to build a thickness controllable second layer by adjusting \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) concentration. Meanwhile, another advantage is regulating mesopore size after etching as a consequence. After GOx encapsulation, the morphology and particle size of composites were monitored by TEM images shown in Figs. 4(a)–4(c). GOx-ZIF-8 was etched to produce mesopores followed by incubation glucose. The corresponding TEM images were exhibited in Figs. 4(d)–4(f). Mesopores can be obviously observed and measured, offering sites for lactase immobilization. Bio-etching process generated pores as well as connection of particles. With the same amount of GOx encapsulated, the concentration of \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) increased gradually in Groups A–C. With higher concentrated precursor was added, more ZIF-8 was produced, resultantly, Group C was more difficult to be etched. After \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) -etching, obvious pores can be seen in Group A and B, and ZIF-8 particles aggregated into much larger pieces, which are displayed in Figs. 4(d) and 4(e). However, milder etching happened with Group C, thereby ZIF-8 structure can be maintained as observed in Fig. 4(f).
\(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) concentration varied from 9.75 to \(48.75\;\mathrm{\mM}\), thereby influencing size of \(\mathrm{GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) as shown in Fig. 4(g). No big
difference was observed in Groups A \( (9.75\;\mathrm{mM}) \) and B (19.50 mM) and the size was kept around \( 200\;\mathrm{nm} \). In comparison to the SEM images in Figs. 4(a)–4(c), the obtained particle size from DLS measurements represents the dynamic hydraulic diameter [37]. While TEM images represent real particle size, generally smaller than hydraulic diameter. Furthermore, the particle aggregation can result in dramatic increase in hydraulic diameter, confirmed by Ref. [38]. Therefore, as concentration increased to \( 48.75\;\mathrm{mM} \) (Group C), thicker Gox-bearing ZIF-8 layer was formed with particle size going up to \( 4\;\upmu\mathrm{m} \) by aggregation.
For comparison, after adding glucose solution for etching, particle size was monitored at different time points as well as among different groups, as shown in Fig. 4(h), suggesting that particle size increased gradually over incubation time. After \( 24\;\mathrm{h} \) etching, particles in Group A possessed the largest mesopores, which can be clearly seen in Fig. 4(d). As the GOx-carrying coating layer became thicker, the etched pores decreased from \( 20.2 \pm 7.0 \) (Group A) to \( 7.0 \pm 1.6\;\mathrm{nm} \) (Group B). The etched pores shrank to only \( 4.1 \pm 0.7\;\mathrm{nm} \) when the concentration of \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) was \( 48.75\;\mathrm{mM} \) (Fig. 4(i)). It is consistent with TEM images in Figs. 4(d)–4(f). Therefore, it is a smart bio-etching strategy for obtaining controllable-sized mesopores in ZIF-8, since the GOx-based etching process can be regulated by adjusting GOx-carrying layer thickness. It is important to tailor the pore size distribution for accommodating biomolecules. Therefore, this strategy holds exciting advantages in macromolecule delivery and other clinical applications.
Interestingly, the mesopores were etched in a rectangle-like shape and small sized pores fused into a larger one (Fig. S3 in the
Interestingly, the mesopores were etched in a rectangle-like shape and small sized pores fused into a larger one (Fig. S3 in the ESM). After etching, the XRD pattern of etched ZIF-8 was collected and compared with simulated pattern, as shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Peaks splitting was observed after etching in mesoporous ZIF-8, which was indicators of the change in symmetry [39]. This can be ascribed to the mesopores in etched ZIF-8.
Since ZIF-8 in this work is designed for drug carriers, especially for potential oral delivery, we evaluated the stability of ZIF-8. ZIF-8 (dispersed in PBS) was immersed in simulated gastric fluid (SGF, \( \mathrm{pH} \approx 2 \)), mimicking the harsh stomach environment. Weight, XRD patterns, and leached \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) were investigated in an intermittent sampling mold at time intervals within \( 8\;\mathrm{h} \) (Fig. S5 in the ESM). XRD patterns comparing ZIF-8 before and after SGF incubation indicate no obvious structural collapse (Fig. S5(a) in the ESM) in the retrieved ZIF-8 samples. However, \( 2.8\%{-}4.9\% \) ZIF-8 weight loss (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM) suggested \( < 5\% \) ZIF-8 was sacrificed, which is consistent with \( 3.5\% \) leached \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) (Fig. S5(c) in the ESM), showing no cumulative behavior with time. It reveals that only a tiny percentage ZIF-8 was sacrificed in SGF and more than \( 95\% \) maintained intact, indicating the feasibility to be applied in harsh gastrointestinal (GI) tract.
## 2.4 Encapsulation of macromolecules by mesoporous ZIF-8 after microlithography
It was calculated by PyMOL that lactase dimension was \( 89.8\;\mathrm{\AA} \times 137.6\;\mathrm{\AA} \times 181.6\;\mathrm{\AA} \) (Fig. 5(a)), which makes it difficult to be encapsulated by most MOFs with pore size \( < 2\;\mathrm{nm} \) [40].
Therefore, mesopores are required for encapsulation and protection. Micro-etching strategy endowed ZIF-8 mesopores as large as \(20\ \mathrm{nm}\) which are suitable for accommodating large-sized biomolecules such as lactase. Subsequently, the immobilization efficiency and amount were quantitatively investigated, as exhibited in Fig. 5(b). Obviously, lactase concentration has an effect on immobilization efficiency, with the maximum efficiency reaching \(67.2\%\pm1.1\%\) , then the figure went down. In perspective of immobilized lactase amount, the highest amount appeared at around \(7.2\,\mathrm{~mg~}\) as the concentration increased to \(25~\mathrm{mg\cdotmL^{-1}}\) . Higher lactase concentration did not show effective promotion due to maximum capacity limit.
Ortho-nitrophenyl- \(\cdot\upbeta\) -galactoside (ONPG) was employed to evaluate lactase activity after immobilization as the substrate followed by traditional lactase activity assessment with obvious color change (Fig. S2 in the ESM), easy to be quantified by spectrophotometry [41]. After obtaining lactase-loaded mesoporous ZIF-8 (meso-ZIF-8), the composites were incubated with ONPG followed by absorbance collection at \(420\,\textrm{n m}\) . In comparison, the direct encapsulation using ZIF-8 particles rather than meso-ZIF-8 was tested as well as shown in Fig. 5(c). MesoZIF-8 is capable to accommodate lactase after bio-etching with 20 nm mesopores generated. The pore size matches computational dimension of lactase. That is the prerequisite of lactase immobilization. In situ coprecipitation can also embed lactase in ZIF-8 particles. However, the lactase activity was seriously destroyed and almost no enzymatic products can be detected (Fig. 5(c)). This is caused by enzymatic activity loss due to denature of lactase molecule, which is a thorny problem in macromolecule immobilization [42]. Significantly, lactase in mesoZIF-8 maintained good activity, which was kept around \(65\%\) compared to free lactase. The decrease in activity may attribute to
mass transfer resistance. Therefore, meso-ZIF-8 avoids complete macromolecule denaturation, and overcomes the problem of severe biocatalytic activity loss.
Moreover, we investigated in vivo enzymatic activity to verify if lactase-ZIF-8 composites could work in complicated intestinal environment. Before in vivo test, oral safety was evaluated in order to avoid severe toxicity. According to Ref. [43], a 4-week body weight monitoring was conducted. The overall growth trend with slight weight fluctuation was observed in both control group and \(10~\mathrm{{\mg\cdotkg^{-1}}}\) ZIF-8 dosed group. No obvious difference was observed, confirming good biocompatibility. Afterwards, in vivo enzymatic activity was assessed using anesthetized mice with clamped small intestine section, as shown in Fig. 5(e). The enzymatic reaction took place in the clamped section, which can verify the in vivo effect in the internal environment. Accordingly, meso-ZIF-8 after lactase loading showed similar substrateconverting capability in vitro compared to free lactase without immobilization (Fig. 5(f)). It is notable that direct encapsulation by ZIF-8 did not show catalysis results while meso-ZIF-8 after bioetching was a good lactase carrier for immobilization, indicating that meso-ZIF-8 has good transnationality in clinical applications. Additionally, histopathological analyses confirmed the absence of toxic effects on main organs collected from mice after 28-day monitoring (Fig. 6). The conclusive results indicate that ZIF-8 system is applicable for upcoming preclinical and clinical trials with minimal risks, due to favorable biocompatibility.
## Conclusion
In summary, we report a facile method using GOx as a microetching precursor to create size-controllable mesopores in ZIF-8. Interestingly, we found the presence of CTAB can greatly affect the morphology and crystallinity of ZIF-8 due to hindered growth in specific plane and orientation. EtOH was selected as a good solvent for obtaining uniform ZIF-8 seeds. GOx was encapsulated in ZIF-8 structure for bioetching by generating an acidic microenvironment. The mesopore size was controllably regulated by adjusting the layer thickness, ranging from 2 to \(10\,\textrm{n m}\) on average and partly reaching up to \(20\ \mathrm{nm}\) . It has been proved that meso-ZIF-8 particles can be applied as carriers for lactase immobilization with good enzymatic activity remaining. The activity was reduced to \(\sim65\%\) due to mass transfer resistance, but showed significant advantages compared to direct coprecipitation using ZIF-8, which did not show biocatalysis effect. Moreover, we verify the substrate converting capability both in vitro and in vivo. The lactase-loaded meso-ZIF-8 exhibited similar enzymatic activity in comparison to free lactase without oral toxicity. This strategy breaks the natural pore size limit of MOF and produces controllable mesopores, which endows great promise and significance for a bundle of applications, such as robust enzyme immobilization, macromolecule delivery and storage.
